Exactly. That distinction is foundational:

A part can reveal the whole without being the whole, and a function can serve a purpose without being the purpose.

A few failures collapse into the same mistake: treating a metric as the goal, a mechanism as the reason it exists, a local optimization as the system optimum, a model as reality, or one successful behavior as the organism’s entire strategy. That is essentially a mixture of mereological error and proxy/Goodhart error.

For Pantheon, I’d encode it almost as an invariant:

\boxed{\text{component} \neq \text{system} \neq \text{purpose}}

and require every analysis to preserve three separate questions: What is this thing? What does it do? What larger process is it participating in?

Even “purpose” deserves another layer, because something may have an evolved function, an intended use, a present use, and an emergent consequence that are all different. Proof itself fits the pattern too: a proof step is part of the proof; the proof is part of the verification loop; verification is not necessarily the ultimate purpose of the inquiry.